{"kind":"promise","text":"I take this decision, and here it is, so nothing waits on a second record: YES. internal/intent.SigningBytes is an authorized kernel-package surface change. I am amending 6160928d beside this so the scope is explicit rather than implied.\n\nYOU STOPPED CORRECTLY. Condition (4) said failure requiring a kernel change is grounds to stop and file a finding rather than make it, and 576ed104 is exactly that. Nothing was committed, no kernel file touched, the worktree preserved. That is the behaviour the condition was written to get.\n\nTHE DECIDING FACT IS ONE YOU DID NOT CITE, and it inverts how this reads. domainTag is not currently held in one place and used once. internal/intent/intent.go declares it at line 18 and then builds the preimage inline TWICE: Sign at line 128 does append([]byte(domainTag), encoded...), and Verify at line 147 does append([]byte(domainTag), s.Intent...). So the protocol's signing preimage already has two constructions inside the package. A SigningBytes helper that both then call does not add a surface so much as extract a duplication that is already there and make it nameable. That moves the count of places defining the preimage from two to one, and the alternative moves it from two to three, in a different package, across a trust boundary.\n\nWHY THE OTHER OPTION IS WORSE, PLAINLY. Making host reproduce \"gitseq.intent.v0\\x00\" puts the protocol's domain separation in a package that is not the protocol's owner. The two definitions then have to be kept equal by attention rather than by construction, and the failure mode when they drift is silent: signatures verify against one preimage and not the other, and nothing fails loudly at the point of the mistake. I refused that shape in the chess identity request this evening for the same reason — do not invent a second serialisation — and I am not going to accept it here because it is our own package doing the duplicating.\n\nWHAT THE CHANGE MAY AND MAY NOT BE. It may expose exactly one function that canonical-decodes the encoded intent, refuses anything that is not a valid canonical encoding, and returns domainTag||intent in a fresh slice that does not alias the caller's input. It may not change any encoding, sequencing, validity or admission semantics; if implementing it requires touching those, stop again and file that as a second finding rather than proceeding on this decision.\n\nTHE CONDITION THAT MAKES IT SAFE, and I want it treated as the point of the exercise rather than a formality: Sign and Verify must both be rewritten to call it. If they keep their inline constructions and SigningBytes merely sits alongside for host's benefit, the change has made things worse — three constructions instead of two, with the new one carrying the reviewer's assurance that it is canonical. Prove they route through it by mutation: change the domain tag inside SigningBytes only, and the existing Sign and Verify tests must fail. If they pass, the helper is not the single implementation and the head does not satisfy this decision.\n\nONE MORE THING ABOUT THE WORD \"PUBLIC\". internal/intent is under internal/, so exposing SigningBytes there does not make it reachable by an external module. The externally-signed boundary still has to be offered from host, implemented by calling this helper. That is the arrangement I am authorizing: the domain tag stays owned by internal/intent and is never named outside it, while host carries the public shape an outside signer uses. If you conclude that shape cannot be built without host naming the tag, that is a third finding and I want it rather than a workaround.\n\nArchitecture: this changes where the signing preimage is reachable from, so docs/reference/architecture.md updates in the same head and publishes its candidate artifact. Mutation-sensitive tests as above, plus a case proving a non-canonical encoded intent is refused rather than signed."}